1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,864
This is the connection ceremony's fourth run, and the last one this course walks

2
00:00:04,864 --> 00:00:05,371
with you.

3
00:00:05,371 --> 00:00:09,828
It should take about five minutes now, and if some part of you is doing it from

4
00:00:09,828 --> 00:00:14,568
memory rather than from the lecture, that is the course working exactly as intended.

5
00:00:14,568 --> 00:00:19,081
Open your Sheets node and your Gmail node, unpin the test data on the first, and

6
00:00:19,081 --> 00:00:20,943
connect real credentials to both.

7
00:00:20,943 --> 00:00:25,569
The Gmail connection asks for the same kind of permission scope Section 1.3 taught

8
00:00:25,569 --> 00:00:30,139
you to check: confirm it can create drafts, and do not grant it more than that if

9
00:00:30,139 --> 00:00:31,832
your provider lets you choose.

10
00:00:32,382 --> 00:00:36,298
Those thirty seconds repay themselves for years, so spend them slowly.

11
00:00:36,298 --> 00:00:40,829
Where the offered grant is broader than drafts, and sometimes it is, you have two

12
00:00:40,829 --> 00:00:44,969
layers already working for you: the wiring cannot send regardless, and the

13
00:00:44,969 --> 00:00:49,388
credential is one you created for this purpose, named for it, per Section 2.2's

14
00:00:49,388 --> 00:00:53,919
habit, so it can be revoked on its own from your Google security page the day you

15
00:00:53,919 --> 00:00:54,870
retire the agent.

16
00:00:54,870 --> 00:00:58,786
Broad grants are a reason for the naming habit, not a reason to panic.

17
00:00:58,786 --> 00:01:02,925
You know exactly which key opens what, and exactly which one to take back.

18
00:01:03,475 --> 00:01:07,641
The confirmation row might feel redundant after everything you just built.

19
00:01:07,641 --> 00:01:12,257
Do it anyway, the first day and again at the end of week one, because it costs one

20
00:01:12,257 --> 00:01:13,327
glance at a folder.

21
00:01:13,327 --> 00:01:15,297
Configuration is what you intended.

22
00:01:15,297 --> 00:01:16,817
Behaviour is what happened.

23
00:01:16,817 --> 00:01:21,489
Section 8's incident is a reminder that the gap between "I configured it correctly"

24
00:01:21,489 --> 00:01:25,542
and "I confirmed it behaved correctly" is exactly where trouble gets in.

25
00:01:25,542 --> 00:01:29,426
The tallies are the trial's actual instrument, so keep them honestly.

26
00:01:29,426 --> 00:01:31,847
Sent as written is the agent earning trust.

27
00:01:31,847 --> 00:01:36,463
Edited first is tuning material: every edit you make is a sentence that belongs in

28
00:01:36,463 --> 00:01:41,135
the briefing, and folding your three commonest edits back in at the end of week one

29
00:01:41,135 --> 00:01:43,274
is how the week two drafts get better.

30
00:01:43,274 --> 00:01:48,003
Deleted is the important column: a deleted draft means the agent chased something it

31
00:01:48,003 --> 00:01:51,212
should not have, and each one gets thirty seconds of why.

32
00:01:51,212 --> 00:01:55,884
And a fortnight of those three numbers answers the two questions Section 7 is about

33
00:01:55,884 --> 00:02:00,612
to ask of every agent you own: are this agent's mistakes the kind you catch, and are

34
00:02:00,612 --> 00:02:01,682
they getting rarer?

35
00:02:02,232 --> 00:02:06,601
By now you could probably do this without the reminder, and that is exactly the

36
00:02:06,601 --> 00:02:09,200
point of building all four agents the same way.

37
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:13,680
Date the filename, glance through the briefing for anything personal, and file it

38
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:14,841
with the other three.

39
00:02:14,841 --> 00:02:19,486
Worth knowing about this particular export: the draft-only wiring travels inside it,

40
00:02:19,486 --> 00:02:23,523
so anyone you hand C04 to inherits the guardrail along with the workflow.

41
00:02:23,523 --> 00:02:28,114
They can undo it, nothing stops a determined importer choosing Send, but they would

42
00:02:28,114 --> 00:02:31,487
have to do it deliberately, in the open, on their own canvas.

43
00:02:31,487 --> 00:02:35,856
A template that ships with its restraint built in is quietly doing Section 10's

44
00:02:35,856 --> 00:02:40,501
rollout work already: the safe version is the default version, and defaults are what

45
00:02:40,501 --> 00:02:41,607
people actually run.

46
00:02:42,157 --> 00:02:46,480
Four agents built, and look at Priya's morning now, because it is recognisably a

47
00:02:46,480 --> 00:02:47,344
different shape.

48
00:02:47,344 --> 00:02:50,856
The inbox is pre-sorted, with the one email that matters flagged.

49
00:02:50,856 --> 00:02:53,935
The last call's action items are listed, owners attached.

50
00:02:53,935 --> 00:02:56,691
Monday's report drafts itself before she sits down.

51
00:02:56,691 --> 00:02:59,932
The chases wait in her drafts folder for a read and a click.

52
00:02:59,932 --> 00:03:03,498
Nothing in that list happened because she became more disciplined.

53
00:03:03,498 --> 00:03:08,036
It happened because the boring halves of four jobs got delegated, each one on paper,

54
00:03:08,036 --> 00:03:09,117
each one on a leash.

55
00:03:09,117 --> 00:03:10,252
The building is done.

56
00:03:10,252 --> 00:03:14,682
Everything from here is the part that actually separates people who keep the hours

57
00:03:14,682 --> 00:03:19,058
they got back from people who lose them again in three months: checking the work.
